From 08b359c5139454f51603e264e1f1e1954e99c2d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(interaction): preserve native air-use prediction and
 timing

Apply corrected Bedrock velocity to Java while gliding so movement
corrections do not preserve stale local motion. Keep protocol 2193 packed
item-use encoding for mining actions embedded in PlayerAuthInput.

Queue mainhand flight rocket use as a standalone inventory transaction
after the auth-input frame that establishes glide start and position.
Keep this queue independent of packed mining actions.

Official Bedrock 1.26.51.1 captures through unchanged Geyser build 1247
show one predicted inventory action reducing three rockets to two. The
transaction uses a negative even legacy request ID, InventoryContainer
29 with the physical hotbar slot, descriptors without stack network IDs,
hand 0, face 255, Unknown trigger, and Failure interaction prediction.
Allocate legacy IDs separately from the odd item-stack-request sequence.
Static analysis of the matching 1.26.51.1 client confirms an independent
counter that starts at -4 and decreases by two.

Keep the flight-rocket inventory and its network IDs unchanged until the server
confirms the result. The captured Geyser route answers this legacy use
with an authoritative InventorySlot update, without ItemStackResponse.
Targeted tests cover input ordering, legacy metadata serialization,
counter isolation, descriptor copies, and the final-rocket debit.

Allocate legacy even request IDs from the inventory owner shared with
native block placement. Keep stack-request IDs independent and verify
interleaved placement/rocket allocation without a second legacy counter.

Extend the same legacy consumption envelope to locally consumed pearl
and wind-charge air use. Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 protocol 2225 captures against local BDS 1.26.60.6
carry a negative even request ID, inventory slot declaration and one-item
debit for both. Held and action descriptors omit network IDs. Retain the
Unknown trigger, Failure prediction and Off cooldown fields from native.

The prior unpredicted throwable transaction causes BDS to send a selecting
PlayerHotbar packet for the used slot. A delayed capture shows that command
returning the client to the pearl slot after it selected a wind charge,
preventing the intended second use. Preserve ordinary throwable packet
timing and authoritative server inventory ownership; only flight rockets
wait for their establishing movement frame. Do not predict a Creative
item debit or alter continuous-use consumption.

A regression checks consecutive native debit requests across a slot
switch, descriptor isolation and an empty final stack. It fails before
the correction with request ID 0 instead of -4. This does not establish
complete native inventory or movement parity or close the separate
reported repeated-rollback issue.

Commit an immediate throwable debit to predicted core inventory only after
sending its transaction. BDS can accept the declared change without a
slot acknowledgement, so waiting for a response leaves the next use with
a stale quantity. Preserve the original stack network ID on the local
remainder; server inventory updates continue to replace predictions. The
resulting equipment packet follows the transaction, matching native order.

A repeated-use control with descriptor prediction alone sent 64-to-63 twice
and the server accepted only two of three attempted throws. The packet-to-
inventory regression now checks repeated debits, preserved local identity,
final-stack removal and send-before-commit ordering.

Accept a sequence-owned local stack-consumption report before ordinary
Java item use. Emit a legacy debit only when local prediction consumed
the item. Keep air use, omitted descriptor network IDs, and Off cooldown
independent of that debit. An absent report preserves older client
behavior. Reports for another sequence cannot select a later prediction.

Original Bedrock 1.26.60.5 GameMode controls record four-to-three and
three-to-two inventory actions on successful pearl use. Cooldown-refused
calls still dispatch but record no action or legacy slot declaration.
Java also sends its use packet while its local cooldown rejects the
callback. An unconditional debit lets BDS accept a near-expiry request
without an inventory response while Java retains its old count. Match
the native local action boundary instead of predicting every attempt.

The regression keeps a refused request unpredicted without advancing
the legacy counter, then verifies the next successful native debit.
It fails before the correction and retains the older-client fallback,
flight ordering, descriptor identity and final-stack tests.

Publish each immediate core-owned throwable debit to the Java inventory.
Java 26.3 MultiPlayerGameMode sends its use packet even when the local
cooldown gate skips item consumption. BDS can accept
the native legacy prediction without returning another quantity packet.
Updating only core then leaves Java's count stale until unrelated authority.

A complete protocol2225/BDS1.26.60.6 direct capture records a final3-to-2
pearl declaration, a renewed cooldown and no quantity update for ten
seconds. BDS settles at2 while Java retains3. The packet regression starts
with that retained Java stack and fails before the publication fix. It
also covers an exhausted final stack. Preserve transaction-before-debit
ordering and original stack identity, and let later server updates replace
predictions. Input continuation and native cooldown fields stay independent.

Retain quantity publication for real debits and clients without the new
local prediction report. A modern cooldown-refused report carries no
action, so it must neither debit core nor publish a temporary lower Java
quantity. The regression also guards this no-publication boundary.
